#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186197940 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fe16334-8f63-56ed-bd58-95e095993b9f" :knowledge-point/title "Jacobian Determinants of Inverse Transformations"}] :question/id #uuid "f794d50a-cb44-5a6f-a922-8438a6192d46" :question/math-academy-id "e-11157" :question/problem "Consider all non-critical points of the transformation $\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (x(u,v),y(u,v))$. The inverse transformation $\\mathbf T^{-1}(x,y)\\to(u(x,y), v(x,y))$ is given by\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T$ in terms of $u$ and $v$." :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T$, denoted\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)}\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(∂(x, y))/(∂(u, v)) &= \\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given $\\mathbf T^{-1}$. So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\dfrac{\\partial (u, v)}{\\partial (x, y)}$, the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T^{-1}$.\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T$ using the relation\n$\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} = \\left(\\dfrac{\\partial (u, v)}{\\partial (x, y)} \\right)^{-1}$.\n\nHere, we have\n\n$$\nu(x,y) = e^{x+y}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nv(x,y) = e^{x-y}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} &= e^{x + y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y}|= e^{x + y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x}|= e^{x - y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}|=-e^{x - y}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(∂(u, v))/(∂(x, y)) &= \\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}e^{x + y} & e^{x + y} \\\\ e^{x - y} & -e^{x - y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= e^{x + y} \\cdot (-e^{x - y}) - e^{x + y} \\cdot e^{x - y} \\\\\n&=-e^{x + y} \\cdot e^{x - y} - e^{x + y} \\cdot e^{x - y} \\\\\n&=-2 \\cdot e^{x + y} \\cdot e^{x - y} \\\\\n&=-2uv\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(∂(x, y))/(∂(u, v)) &= ((∂(u, v))/(∂(x, y)))^{-1} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{2uv}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186197970 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4179839d-85c4-5325-9293-e332bd209597" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Inverse Function Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "217fe5bd-4ea4-57b9-8772-bb6e517207bd" :question/math-academy-id "e-12416" :question/problem "The function $f(x)$, whose graph is shown above, has a stationary point at $x=0$ and is continuously differentiable everywhere except at $x=-3$. Which of the following statements **must** be true according to the inverse function theorem?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4149/Source/Images/af9f81048aa4f33fd0c1b0f79d35ca30.png)\n\n1. $f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $x=-3$\n2. $f^{-1}(y)$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $y=0$\n3. The derivative of $f^{-1}(y)$ at $y=-1.1$ equals $\\dfrac{1}{f'(2)}$" :question/worked-solution "The inverse function theorem for single-variable functions states the following:\n\n> *If $f: U \\to \\Bbb R$ is continuously differentiable on an open set $U\\subseteq \\mathbb R$, $f(a) = b$ and $f'(a)\\neq 0$ for $a\\in U$, then*\n> - *$f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $a$,*\n> - *$f^{-1}$, the inverse of $f$, is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $b$, and*\n> - *the derivative of $f^{-1}$ is given by*\n> $\\left(f^{-1}\\right)'(b) = \\dfrac{1}{f'(a)} =\\dfrac{1}{f'(f^{-1}(b))}\\,$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that $f$ is not continuous at $x = -3$. So, the theorem can't be applied in this situation.\n- Statement II is also false. Notice that $y = 0 = f(0)$. However, the function $f$ does *not* satisfy the conditions for the inverse function theorem at $x = 0$ since $f'(0) = 0$. So, the theorem can't be applied in this situation.\n- Statement III is true. Notice that $y = -1.1 = f(2)$, and the function $f$ satisfies the conditions for the inverse function theorem at $x = 2$. Indeed,$f$ is continuously differentiable on an open set containing $x=2$, for example, $(0,4)$, andthe derivative of $f$ is nonzero at $x = 2$, i.e., $f'(2) \\neq 0$.Therefore, by the inverse function theorem, the derivative of $f^{-1}(y)$ at $y = -1.1$ equals $\\dfrac{1}{f'(2)}$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198161 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf5e8b36-38f0-5dd9-8b76-82392e593b73" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Critical Points of a Transformation"}] :question/id #uuid "d154ea1a-bb3c-510d-abe3-dc1cf51558b4" :question/math-academy-id "e-7127" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $\\mathbf{T}$, where $\\mathbf T$ is defined as\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T$, denoted\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)}\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(∂(x, y))/(∂(u, v)) &= \\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the locations of the critical points of $\\mathbf T$, we need to solve\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} = 0\n$$\n\nHere, we have\n\n$$\nx(u,v) = u^2-v^2\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny(u,v) = u+v\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} &= 2u & \\frac{∂x}{∂v}|=-2v \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u}|= 1 & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}|= 1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(∂(x, y))/(∂(u, v)) &= \\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}2u & -2v \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= 2(u + v)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} =0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(∂(x, y))/(∂(u, v)) &= 0 \\\\\n2(u + v) &= 0 \\\\\nu &= -v\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the critical points of $T$ consist of those points that lie on the line $u=-v$ in the $uv$-plane."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198928 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf5e8b36-38f0-5dd9-8b76-82392e593b73" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Critical Points of a Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b9cc660-2fe6-53b6-a36c-17866301f5f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $v = u$." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197804} {:answer/id #uuid "b7d2fb13-b4d9-5f08-b9b4-bfdfca42e7c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $v = 0$." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197805} {:answer/id #uuid "d9383f7a-f088-58a3-9bfd-cedbbb342dea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $u = 0$." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197806} {:answer/id #uuid "ec779a3b-5ffa-5d48-ae98-598484862e65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the circle $u^{2} + v^{2} = 1$." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197807} {:answer/id #uuid "0a730b13-0915-554c-94b9-eec1d8da6fe0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the hyperbola $u^{2} - v^{2} = 1$." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197808}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b9cc660-2fe6-53b6-a36c-17866301f5f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $v = u$." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197804} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac2300ea-1a1a-5ab6-8ab4-8f3977dc0a9c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197803}] :question/id #uuid "1a6af403-e6c8-5c17-8e90-e6cdab8d3462" :question/math-academy-id "q-81169" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of $T$, where $T$ is defined as\n\n$T: \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}uv \\\\ u + v\\end{bmatrix}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198929 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fe16334-8f63-56ed-bd58-95e095993b9f" :knowledge-point/title "Jacobian Determinants of Inverse Transformations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b387d35-4fed-517c-8ba8-94b95e204b3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2v}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197810} {:answer/id #uuid "a24b045e-7cf0-503f-be5a-8ba593a2c54a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197811} {:answer/id #uuid "cc13729e-a24d-5dd5-b9f5-e0d9e96164bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4v}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197812} {:answer/id #uuid "20e4ae68-df4f-5dff-8476-07d9ce71f8b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197813} {:answer/id #uuid "4fcff66d-5fe2-5e46-b1a7-c1f159cd365e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197814}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cc13729e-a24d-5dd5-b9f5-e0d9e96164bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4v}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197812} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf356fa4-81bb-57dc-9099-343ca13a886b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197809}] :question/id #uuid "24c29280-75c6-50b5-ad20-b15d70578fce" :question/math-academy-id "q-81305" :question/problem "Consider all non-critical points of the transformation $T: (u, v) → (x(u, v), y(u, v))$. The inverse transformation $T^{-1}(x, y) → (u(x, y), v(x, y))$ is given by\n\n$T^{-1}: \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}2xy \\\\ \\frac{y}{x}\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $T$ in terms of $u$ and $v$."}]}
